{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "HTML Formatting"}, {"type": "position", "content": 6}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 2}, {"type": "level", "content": "A1.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use when you are writing a text with HTML."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe common simple incidents at work in a simple spoken or written situation with a supportive interlocutor. Assess the incident of data in the macrosystem processes, based on the data dictionary to consolidate intervening data, based in established parameters."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Communicates effectively in English about the communicative topics for this level through the use of appropriate basic vocabulary and grammatical structures, and with intelligible pronunciation.  Writes simple and short texts about programming tools and solutions to a client's requirement."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n2le02ob06re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Indefinite Pronouns in Negative Statements and Questions"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "We use <span class=\"strong\">indefinite pronouns  </span>to talk about a person or a thing without referring to it directly. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "We normally use <span class=\"strong\"> indefinite pronouns</span> to talk about people or things. For <span class=\"strong\"> people</span>, indefinite pronouns end in <span class=\"strong\">\u2013body</span> or in <span class=\"strong\">\u2013one</span>. For <span class=\"strong\"> things</span>, indefinite pronouns end in <span class=\"strong\">\u2013thing</span>. Let's take a look at the following chart of <span class=\"strong\"> indefinite pronouns </span> that we can use in negative statements: "}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":3,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"People\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Things\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Anybody  <br/>Anyone\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Anything \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Nobody<br/> No one\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Nothing \\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Examples: <br/> <span class=\"italic\">  <span class=\"strong\"> No one </span> knows how HTML works. <br/> There is <span class=\"strong\"> nothing </span> we can do to improve the code.  </span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "We can also ask questions with indefinite pronouns when we do not need to specify the thing or person we are talking about. Let's take a look at the indefinite pronouns that we can use when asking questions:"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":5,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"People\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Things\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Anybody <br/> Anyone\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Anything\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Nobody <br/>No one\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Nothing\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Everybody<br/> Everyone \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Everything\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Somebody <br/>Someone\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Something\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Examples: <br/> <span class=\"italic\">  Does <span class=\"strong\"> somebody </span> know about HTML here? <br/> Do I have to include <span class=\"strong\"> everything </span> in the code? </span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Can someone tell me the commands for these texts?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Some of you told me that nothing appeared when you tried to follow the strike and monospaced commands."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Is there anything else we are missing?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Nobody mentioned superscript and subscript texts in the blog, so I'm going to explain them quickly."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for a website you like."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Identify the different ways in which the text appears. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Write 5 examples of the different styles using the commands you learned in this learning material."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share it with a partner."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I identify the different styles in a HTML text."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I write examples of the HTML commands I learned in the learning material."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I use the proper structure to write HTML texts."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Commands For Writing Different Types of Text in HTML"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "HTML has different commands to write text."}, {"type": "text", "content": "In the chart below we can appreciate some common commands used in HTML to write text."}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":12,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"COMMAND\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"DESCRIPTION\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"< b>...< /b>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"BOLD TEXT\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"< i>...</i >\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"ITALIC TEXT\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"< u>...< /u>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"UNDERLINED TEXT\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"< strike>...< /strike>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"THIN LINE THROUGH THE TEXT\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"< tt>...< /tt>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"MONOSPACED TEXT\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"< sup>...< /sup>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"SUPERSCRIPT TEXT\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"< sub>...< /sub>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"SUBSCRIPT TEXT\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"< ins>...< /ins>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"INSERTED TEXT\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"< del>...< /del>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"DELETED TEXT\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"< big>...< /big>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"LARGER TEXT\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"< small>...< /small>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"SMALLER TEXT\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}"}, {"type": "example", "content": "But in monospaced font, we get all letters to occupy the same space by using the < tt >\u2026< /tt > tags."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Inserted and deleted texts. These two usually go together and we use them to indicate a text we insert and one we delete. The first one is underlined and the second one is crossed out. The tags are simple: < ins >\u2026< /ins > and < del >\u2026< /del >."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": " word processor"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a software that edits or formats a text in an electronic device  "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: When we use HTML or any other word processor, we can use commands to make the text appear differently. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le02ob06aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "appear "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to come into being, to look in certain way "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: When we use HTML or any other word processor, we can use commands to make the text appear differently. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le02ob06aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "above "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "over something, over a point of reference"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adverb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Nobody mentioned superscript and subscript texts in the blog, so I'm going to explain them quickly. We use them when we want a text to appear a character's height above or below the other characters, respectively. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le02ob06aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "respectively "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "in the order given or mentioned"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adverb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Nobody mentioned superscript and subscript texts in the blog, so I'm going to explain them quickly. We use them when we want a text to appear a character's height above or below the other characters, respectively. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le02ob06aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "underline "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to put a line mark under a word"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: For bold we use the < b >\u2026< /b > tags; for italic < i >\u2026 < /i>; and for underlined text < u >\u2026 < /u >."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le02ob06aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "width "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the measurement of something from side to side"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Monospaced font refers to a type of font where all letters have the same width. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le02ob06aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "id": 0, "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome"}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe8n2le02ob06re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe8n2le02ob06re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<span class=\"strong\"> Professor:</span> Today in class, we are reviewing HTML formatting. So, when we use HTML or any other word processor, we can use commands to make the text appear differently. In the class blog, many of you mentioned three common ways in which text appears: Bold, italic or underlined text. Can someone tell me the commands for these texts? <br/><span class=\"strong\"> Annie:</span> Sure! For bold we use the < b >\u2026< /b > tags; for italic < i >\u2026 < /i>; and for underlined text < u >\u2026 < /u >.\n<br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Professor:</span>That's right. Some of you told me that nothing appeared when you tried to follow the strike and monospaced commands. Can anybody explain what these are and how to write them?"}, {"time": "68000", "image": "enpe8n2le02ob06re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<span class=\"strong\"> Annie:</span> Yes! We use strike text when we want to draw a line over a word or sentence. For this, we use the < strike >\u2026< /strike > tags. And monospaced font refers to a type of font where all letters have the same width. As we all know, most fonts are variable-width font as letters such as \u2018i' and \u2018l' take less space than \u2018o' or \u2018b'. But in monospaced font, we get all letters to occupy the same space by using the < tt >\u2026< /tt > tags.\n<br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Professor:</span>Perfect. Now, nobody mentioned superscript and subscript texts in the blog, so I'm going to explain them quickly. We use them when we want a text to appear a character's height above or below the other characters, respectively. For the first one we use < sup >\u2026 < /sup> and for the second one < sub >\u2026 < /sub>. Is there anything else we are missing?"}, {"time": "151000", "image": "enpe8n2le02ob06re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<span class=\"strong\"> Annie:</span> Yes, inserted and deleted texts. These two usually go together and we use them to indicate a text we insert and one we delete. The first one is underlined and the second one is crossed out. The tags are simple: < ins >\u2026< /ins > and < del >\u2026< /del >. Is that everything?\n<br/>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Professor:</span>You forgot larger and smaller texts. As their names suggest, we use them to make a piece of text look bigger or smaller than the rest of the text. We use the following commands respectively: < big >\u2026 < /b > and < small >\u2026 < /small >. Is there something else someone wants to add? \u2026"}], "coverimg": "enpe8n2le02ob06re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "title": ""}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"video_src": "enpe8n2le02ob06re5vsc01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "", "id": 0, "text": ""}]}, "type": "video"}], "type": "video", "_data": [], "id": 3, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can understand written information about the ways text a